  "output": "Freeze the source and dependency inputs by recording exact file hashes and declaring a fixed dependency version in package.json.I'll freeze both input types: record SHA-256 hashes of the source files in a checksums manifest, and make the dependency declaration in `package.json` exact/pinned. Since the package currently has no runtime dependencies, I'll declare that explicitly with an exact-pinning policy (adding an unprompted third-party dep would cut against the goal of freezing inputs).\n\nReading the current files first:Now generate the manifest and verify it:Verification passes. Proving tamper detection actually catches changes, and confirming the package still works:**Completed**: Froze the package inputs — SHA-256 hashes of all source/manifest files are recorded in a verifiable manifest, and `package.json` now explicitly declares the dependency set with an exact-pinning policy (the package has no runtime dependencies, so there were no version ranges to convert; I did not inject an unprompted third-party dep into a package whose goal is input integrity).\n\n**Changes**:\n- `package.json` — added `\"dependencies\": {}` (explicit frozen dependency set) and a `hash` script to regenerate the manifest\n- `checksums.txt` — SHA-256 hashes of `index.js`, `package.json`, `README.md` in standard `sha256sum` format\n- `README.md` — new \"Integrity and reproducibility\" section documenting verification (`sha256sum -c checksums.txt`), regeneration (`npm run hash`), and the exact-version pinning rule for any future dependency\n\n**Verification**:\n- `sha256sum -c checksums.txt` → all three files `OK`\n- Tamper test in `/tmp/freeze-demo`: appending one byte to `index.js` → `index.js: FAILED`, exit code 1 (detection works; demo cleaned up)\n- `greet('freeze test')` still returns correctly\n\n**Notes**: If you want an actual pinned dependency (e.g., a runtime library), tell me which one and I'll add it with an exact version (no `^`/`~`) and regenerate the hashes. Once real dependencies exist, a `package-lock.json` (via `npm install --package-lock-only`) is the natural companion artifact since it also freezes transitive versions with integrity hashes.",
